Unzoom viewport- and container-percentage units when resolving font-size
(WEBKIT_FONT_SIZE_UNIT_UNZOOM=1, default off).

Users reported db.no (dagbladet.no) and vg.no "not displaying properly".
Device-reproduced on beta7-626: headlines render exactly 3x too large and
overflow the right edge, while every non-text box on the same page -- images,
cards, buttons -- is laid out correctly for the 360px viewport.

Measured with injected probes by rendered geometry, not just computed style
(Xperia 10 II, dpr 3, innerWidth 360, innerHeight 840; container 300x200):

    height:100vh    -> 840px  correct      font-size:84px   ->  84px correct
    height:10cqw    ->  30px  correct      font-size:10vh   -> 252px (exp 84)
    height:10cqh    ->  20px  correct      font-size:10vw   -> 108px (exp 36)
                                           font-size:10cqw  ->  90px (exp 30)
                                           font-size:10cqh  ->  60px (exp 20)

The error factor is exactly the device scale factor, and font-size is the only
affected property: a sweep of 20 others (line-height, letter-spacing,
text-indent, border-width, border-radius, padding, margin, width, height,
column-gap, flex-basis, text-decoration-thickness, stroke-width, perspective,
background-size, box-shadow, text-shadow, transform, ...) all measure 1.00x.

User agent is not involved: all five built-in UA profiles, including two
desktop ones, produce a byte-identical 144.309601px for dagbladet's lead
headline, and the probes above are injected elements that do not depend on the
served HTML.

Both reported sites size headlines with *container* units, so the container
path matters as much as the viewport one:

    dagbladet  h2.headline:has(.auto-font-size-line)
                 { font-size: var(--lab-auto-font-size, 5cqi) }
               --lab-auto-font-size is unset, so the 5cqi fallback renders,
               at 144.31px instead of 48.10px
    vg.no      ._avatarInitials { font-size: 40cqw } -> 432px, not 144px

Cause. Atlantic implements the 3x UI scale as page zoom -- WPEQtView calls
webkit_web_view_set_zoom_level(view, deviceScaleFactor) -- so both the frame
view's size for CSS viewport units and a query container's contentBox are in
zoomed (device) pixels. That is harmless for ordinary lengths, because the
viewport- and container-percentage cases return their result directly and so
skip the "* conversionData.zoom()" that px/em units receive.

font-size does not work that way. It resolves through
CSSToLengthConversionData::copyForFontSize(), which sets zoom = 1 because a
*specified* font size is stored unzoomed and multiplied by style.usedZoom()
afterwards, in computedFontSizeFromSpecifiedSize(). A viewport or container
unit reaches that multiplication already zoomed, so the zoom lands twice.

Fix. When resolving a font size, divide these units by style.usedZoom() -- the
exact factor computedFontSizeFromSpecifiedSize() will multiply back in -- so
the stored specified size is unzoomed like every other specified size and the
two cancel. Two call sites: adjustValueForPageZoom() itself, reached only from
the viewport-percentage cases, and resolveContainerUnit(), which explicitly
skips that helper when computingFontSize().

Scope. px/em/rem/ch/ex and the absolute units never reach either site, so they
are untouched. The existing rangeZoomOption/evaluationTimeZoomEnabled branch is
left alone and is inert on this port anyway (EvaluationTimeZoomEnabled defaults
to false off Cocoa); the new branch is deliberately independent of it.

--- a/Source/WebCore/style/values/primitives/StyleLengthResolution.cpp
+++ b/Source/WebCore/style/values/primitives/StyleLengthResolution.cpp
@@ -43,12 +43,63 @@
 #include "RenderView.h"
 #include "StylePrimitiveNumericTypes+Conversions.h"
 #include "StylePrimitiveNumericTypes+Evaluation.h"
+#include <stdlib.h>
 
 namespace WebCore {
 namespace Style {
 
+// Atlantic: the 3x UI scale is implemented as page zoom (WPEQtView sets the web
+// view's zoom level to the device scale factor), so the sizes these percentage
+// units resolve against -- the frame view's size for CSS viewport units, and a
+// query container's contentBox -- are in zoomed, i.e. device, pixels.
+//
+// That is harmless for ordinary lengths: the viewport- and container-percentage
+// cases return their result directly and so skip the "* conversionData.zoom()"
+// that px/em receive, which leaves e.g. "height: 100vh" and "height: 10cqw"
+// correct.
+//
+// font-size is different. It resolves through
+// CSSToLengthConversionData::copyForFontSize(), which sets zoom = 1 because a
+// *specified* font size is stored unzoomed and multiplied by style.usedZoom()
+// afterwards, in computedFontSizeFromSpecifiedSize(). A viewport or container
+// unit reaches that multiplication already zoomed, so the zoom lands twice and
+// the text comes out exactly deviceScaleFactor times too large.
+//
+// Device-reproduced on vg.no ("font-size: 40cqw" on an avatar rendering at
+// 432px instead of 144px) and dagbladet.no / db.no, whose headlines resolve
+// "var(--lab-auto-font-size, 5cqi)" to 144.31px instead of 48.10px and overflow
+// the viewport, while every non-text box on the same page is laid out
+// correctly.
+//
+// WEBKIT_FONT_SIZE_UNIT_UNZOOM=1 divides these units by style.usedZoom() when
+// resolving a font size, so the stored specified size is unzoomed like every
+// other specified size and the two cancel. Default off.
+//
+// This is deliberately independent of the rangeZoomOption /
+// evaluationTimeZoomEnabled path below, which is inert on this port
+// (EvaluationTimeZoomEnabled defaults to false off Cocoa).
+static bool unzoomFontSizeUnits()
+{
+    static const bool enabled = [] {
+        const char* env = getenv("WEBKIT_FONT_SIZE_UNIT_UNZOOM");
+        return env && *env && *env != '0';
+    }();
+    return enabled;
+}
+
 static double adjustValueForPageZoom(double dimension, const CSSToLengthConversionData& conversionData)
 {
+    if (unzoomFontSizeUnits() && conversionData.computingFontSize()) {
+        // Divide by the same factor computedFontSizeFromSpecifiedSize() will
+        // multiply back in, so the two cancel exactly.
+        if (auto* style = conversionData.style()) {
+            float usedZoom = style->usedZoom();
+            if (usedZoom > 0)
+                return dimension / usedZoom;
+        }
+        return dimension;
+    }
+
     if (conversionData.rangeZoomOption() != CSS::RangeZoomOptions::Unzoomed)
         return dimension;
 
@@ -257,7 +308,10 @@
                 auto widthOrHeight = physicalAxis == CQ::Axis::Width ? containerRenderer->contentBoxWidth() : containerRenderer->contentBoxHeight();
                 auto adjustedWidthOrHeight = widthOrHeight.toDouble();
 
-                if (!conversionData.computingFontSize())
+                // The container's contentBox is a zoomed layout value, so a
+                // font size resolved from it needs the same unzooming the
+                // viewport units get -- see unzoomFontSizeUnits() above.
+                if (!conversionData.computingFontSize() || unzoomFontSizeUnits())
                     adjustedWidthOrHeight = adjustValueForPageZoom(adjustedWidthOrHeight, conversionData);
 
                 return adjustedWidthOrHeight * value / 100;
